WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.112
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.112 --> 00:00:04.820
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.200
Welcome to our talk today.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.440
My name is Jelena
Pjesivac-Grbovic.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.550
I'm a staff software
engineer at Google

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.750
and one of the technical leads
on Cloud Dataflow product.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.910
Today, we're going to look
at the data processing

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:20.830
tools and their performance
and how the cloud processing

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.610
tools, Dataproc and
Dataflow, achieve

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.270
the excellent and optimal
performance out of the box.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.800
So data processing performance--
why do we care about it?

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.860
Well, it matters
because it affects

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.340
the latency of our result.
So to achieve our result,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.610
we have to spend time
running the pipeline

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.030
and depending how
efficient it is,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.150
it affects when our
result is available.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.670
Depending on what
your application is,

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.350
the time it takes to
get to the solution

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:57.420
can be make it or break
it event for product.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.460
Another reason why the
performance matters

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.780
is the costs.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.990
Your applications run
on the real hardware.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.670
Running on real
hardware costs money,

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:09.690
whether you use the cloud
or you use the on-premise.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.320
So if it's inefficient, you're
literally wasting money.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.360
So that's important thing.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.060
And combining these
two, performance

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.300
can make data analysis
feasible or unfeasible.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.860
If it's too slow,
we don't need it.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.420
If it's too expensive,
we cannot afford it.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.590
So performance is really
critical in making it possible

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.485
for us to do a data analysis.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.020
So what are the factors
that affect the performance?

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.204
There are many of
them, and first set

00:01:42.204 --> 00:01:43.620
of factors we are
going to look at

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.100
are related to your application,
essentially the things that

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.650
are sort of in your control.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.600
First thing is data
shape and distribution.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.420
Are your old data records
approximately the same size?

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.260
Or do they vary a lot, right?

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.100
If you think of someone
processing a social graph,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.230
a node that contains
the social butterfly

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.650
will be orders of magnitudes
bigger than a node containing

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.320
an average person.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.780
The other thing that's sort
similar but slightly different

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.370
is the per-record computation
cost distribution.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.140
Do all records cost the same?

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.490
Like, does it take
same amount of CPU time

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.490
to process each of your
records, or again, there

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.960
is a big difference?

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.530
If you think of something
that's processing map tiles,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.990
like the processing a
map tile on Antarctica

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.900
versus processing a map
tile in San Francisco

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.630
probably incurs different
computation costs.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.070
And finally, within
application space,

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:46.300
the one factor is
implementation efficiency,

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.820
how well you were at
like, optimizing it,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.650
how well you were using the
APIs that are given to you,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.090
how is your memory management,
and things like that.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.902
So to address the
application factors,

00:03:00.902 --> 00:03:02.610
there is a traditional
performance tuning

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.710
and optimisation process.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.400
We profile our data and we
profile the application.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.250
Once we understand our
data and application,

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.920
we can perfectly split our
data, partition it for execution

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.980
and parallel, and we can
perfectly schedule those tasks.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.440
Of course, this perfect,
take with a grain of salt.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.050
That we can perfectly
schedule the task,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.030
Java will execute and produce
a result, and we'll be happy.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.160
If that was it, like,
we're done right?

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.200
We've computed
everything we needed.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.360
But in reality,
this is not the end.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.600
We run our data processing
pipelines regularly,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.910
sometimes even continually.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.910
So this process is a process
that has to be repeated.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:47.670
And it sort of works, except
expertise is required.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.620
The person who can do
the profiling and data

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.110
and application is not
easy to find, right?

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.230
And the thing is, if you
have one application,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:58.530
sure you can maybe afford
someone who will be your expert

00:03:58.530 --> 00:03:59.830
and make it run great.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.580
But if you have hundreds,
you really can't scale it.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.260
So what happens then is
people copy tuning parameters

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.740
from the one that was tuned.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.699
And it keeps going,
and you copy it and you

00:04:12.699 --> 00:04:15.240
hope that it's going to achieve
the same performance benefit.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.279
It did for original pipeline.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:18.060
But guess what?

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.380
It usually doesn't.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.130
And especially over
time, it gets worse.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.660
And you may not be
even aware of it.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:32.470
So the other aspect of where the
traditional performance tuning

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.450
and optimization
sort of break down

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.520
is that it's not
always possible.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.880
If you have a complex
pipeline with multiple stages,

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.570
you may not be able to reliably
profile intermediate stages,

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:48.500
because that data depends on
actually data that came in.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.230
And if that changes,
the properties

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.140
of the intermediate data change.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:56.890
So you really can't
always benefit from this.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.680
And finally, even if we
did everything perfectly

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.420
and we had a full knowledge, we
are running on the real system,

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.690
on the machines that will have
some things going on them.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.030
And they will not
all behave the same.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.400
And that brings us to
the execution environment

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.335
as a factor of our
data performance.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:21.090
So a big part of execution
environment factors

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.200
are workers.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.121
Some workers will be fast,
some workers will be slow.

00:05:25.121 --> 00:05:25.620
Why?

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:26.140
Who knows.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.820
There are many reasons why
workers can be slow or faster.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:34.530
For example, most
of the work now

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.390
days runs in a VM that
share the physical machine,

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.130
and depending on the
quality of isolation,

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.700
one can affect the other.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.970
The other thing that is
important is the networking.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:50.260
So network conditions
are even less--

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.960
you can even less
isolate the networks

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.190
in terms of the impact.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:57.580
And you know, someone else
using the same network you do

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.020
will affect your performance.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.510
And finally, this is
like resource contention.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.850
And this can be on
any possible level.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.740
It can be resource
contention in your code.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.310
It can be resource contention
in a worker that you're running.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.320
It can be resource
contention on a VM,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.400
or it can be resource contention
on a service that you rely on.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.950
Imagine you're reading a
service that can support

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.800
certain amount of QPS, right?

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.500
And then there is a big user
that comes and pretty much

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:26.730
uses up everything.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:31.760
Your job-- all the jobs will
slow down because of that.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:34.400
So the impact of uneven
worker performance

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.825
gets worse with scale.

00:06:35.825 --> 00:06:38.440
Amdahl's law tells
us essentially that.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.900
And the more parallelism
we have, the more serious

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.940
bottleneck the serial part of
our application have become.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.850
So how do we deal with
execution environment?

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.550
Well, I mean, you can
remove slow machines.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:57.190
That's one.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.870
But that's not something you can
do on demand and automatically.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:04.230
What people do is usually
avoidance techniques, so things

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.840
like a backup or worker resets.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.240
So if your application
can detect that something

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.340
is going slow, that
a worker is slow,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.990
it can choose to use
a different worker

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.350
or it can schedule the work
done on that worker to someone

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.720
else who is faster.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.260
And that's almost
it from the dealing

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.630
with execution environment.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.920
Another factor that's related
to the execution environment

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.150
factor in this is the
system complexity.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:35.960
Your application uses an SDK.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:39.460
And when it runs, that SDK
runs on some kind of a worker

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.080
harness, your thing
that makes your API

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.570
calls and puts them in a system.

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:47.660
And that runs in some
kind of a container that

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:49.990
runs on a virtual
machine that may

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.790
be running multiple containers.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.220
And that runs on
a physical machine

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:58.170
that may be running
multiple virtual machines.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.629
And if you're doing distributed
application, guess what?

00:08:00.629 --> 00:08:02.170
There are multiple
physical machines,

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:04.760
and they are connected
by a network.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:08.600
So anywhere in this stack,
something going wrong

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.270
or something being slow
affects everything above it.

00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:21.200
Yet we are not done
with the factors

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.670
that affect the performance.

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:24.140
We have our dependencies.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:28.220
And I mention them, but we will
call them out here explicitly.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.160
So one set of dependencies is
related to the client libraries

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:32.120
you use.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.490
If you use inefficient
implementation of something,

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.220
it will affect performance
of your application.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.200
And often client
libraries we use

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.130
are like I/O libraries,
your connector to something,

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:48.510
convenience libraries, like
a parser or a formatter,

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:50.210
and then some domain
specific libraries,

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.640
maybe some kind of a
math library or something

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.210
that you may need to actually
compute what you need.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.650
System libraries are similar.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:04.350
Your VMs and all this comes
with some predefined images.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:07.650
And they can have a good set of
libraries are not so good set

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.550
of libraries.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:14.200
OK, so to summarize,
the factors that

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:18.250
affect application performance,
we've seen they can be static,

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.650
like a system libraries
or something, or dynamic,

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:23.260
like the things that happen
at the runtime, which

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:27.540
were our worker and execution
environment, for example.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.070
The data processing tools need
to adapt to the application

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.020
and the environment.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:35.940
And the underlying
system and dependencies

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:37.640
affect application performance.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:45.270
So with this, we'll look at
the two specific Google Cloud

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.430
processing tools,
Dataflow and Dataproc,

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.620
and how they tackle
different aspects

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:54.600
of the different factors that
affect application performance.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:58.709
So first we will look
at the Cloud Dataflow,

00:09:58.709 --> 00:10:00.250
and how it deals
with the application

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:03.540
specific dependencies
and environment.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.850
And then we look at
the Dataproc and how

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:10.490
it deals with the system
level improvements.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.800
So let's switch the gear
and talk a little bit

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.250
about the Cloud Dataflow.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.860
Cloud Dataflow is a fully
managed, no-ops data

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.120
processing.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.270
It offers unified batch
streaming processing power

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:26.210
and it uses open
source programming

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.490
model using Apache Beam.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.372
It can intelligently design
to scale to millions of QPS

00:10:31.372 --> 00:10:33.080
if you're using it in
the streaming mode,

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:37.369
and also can run really
large batch jobs.

00:10:37.369 --> 00:10:39.410
So how does the working
with Dataflow looks like?

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:42.380
You wake up and you write
your Apache Beam pipeline.

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:45.860
Then you take it and you submit
it to Cloud Dataflow service.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.810
The Dataflow service,
and it's behind,

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.720
has a bunch of back
ends that provide

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.807
many different services, some
of which we'll talk about,

00:10:53.807 --> 00:10:55.640
that are sitting there
waiting for your jobs

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.170
to come in schedule.

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:58.610
So the service
will schedule here

00:10:58.610 --> 00:10:59.990
job on one of these back ends.

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:03.140
This back end will take
your Beam pipeline,

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:06.410
run it through a graph
optimizer, do some annotations,

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:08.570
some analysis on it,
and it will come up

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.480
with a initial
configuration for your job,

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:13.820
and will start resources
for you on which you

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.140
will execute that job.

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:17.150
So it will start
GCs, it will start

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:19.370
allocate the buckets
and everything else

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.590
that you may need for this job.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:23.000
Your job will start to run.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.520
You'll access your inputs.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:29.230
They can be on GCP or they
can be external sources.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:31.700
You'll read them,
you'll process,

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:35.910
process, process, produce
the output, and you're done.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.520
So this is our workflow.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.530
So what are the benefits of it?

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.150
Well, it's fully managed
and auto-configured.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.510
You don't have to worry about
deploying your packages,

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.880
your JAR files,
preallocating VMs.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:51.480
You just submit, essentially,
your job to the service

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.740
and it runs.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.020
And all the resources
that your pipeline uses

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.450
are allocated on demand.

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:05.130
We also do automatic
optimized execution graph.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:08.410
So we'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:11.020
We also mitigate the stragglers
dynamically, using something

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:13.950
we call dynamic
worker balancing.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.920
And we can automatically
scale at runtime

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.300
to match the throughput
your application needs.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:25.010
So if you look at these
four benefits, three of them

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.890
are trying to address
the factors that

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:31.280
affect the performance.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:35.330
So Apache Beam looking at the
optimizations-- the pipeline

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:37.340
graph versus execution graph.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:39.800
What Apache Beam gives your
is very intuitive programming

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:44.060
model that allows you to express
the complex computation simply.

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:44.780
That's great.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.700
Except that's really
not the way you

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.230
should be running
this application.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.930
So once we have
your pipeline, we

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:53.660
run through many of the
optimizations algorithms

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:56.480
to provide the optimal
execution graph

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:59.240
that we can run that will
produce the output that you

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:00.480
want.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.220
So this also, like
in this process,

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.920
while we are optimizing,
we try to estimate

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.140
what is the initial
configuration for your job

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:10.700
that we think will run it well.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.710
And now, let's look
at the dynamic factors

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.360
and look at the stragglers.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:22.940
So what is the straggler?

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:25.220
In this framework, the
straggler is a task

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:28.820
that takes longer to complete
than the average completion

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:31.280
time across all the workers.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.430
So here is a graph,
and I'll explain it.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:37.670
So first, on our
x-axis, we have time.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:39.710
On y-axis, we have workers.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:43.160
This is a gun chart of the state
of the workers at the given

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:44.100
time.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:47.180
So each of horizontal
lines represents

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.940
a worker, and the tasks
that were running on it

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:53.210
at the given time, like
if you did a vertical cut.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.480
So you can see
that in this graph,

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:57.995
the worker finished
the chart, it

00:13:57.995 --> 00:14:01.100
will start working on the
next one, finish the task,

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:02.987
start working on the
next time, and things

00:14:02.987 --> 00:14:04.070
are going pretty smoothly.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:09.400
There's not huge variability
between the end times.

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.290
The execution with the
stragglers looks different.

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:15.020
Like, some of the tasks
are taking much longer

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.670
than the others.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.770
And they're affecting
our end time.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.900
Do these things happen in the
production, in your real jobs?

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:24.010
Oh yeah, they do.

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:27.700
So this is a typical
MapReduce liked pipeline,

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.060
where you have a read
operation, you group it by key,

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:32.680
and you write it.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.030
This is a job with 400 workers
running for about 20 minutes.

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:40.060
And if you look at what
those 400 workers worked on,

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:43.960
there are these huge gaps
where many workers sat idly,

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:47.440
while few other workers
were trying to finish.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.330
And this is a big
resource waste.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:56.040
So to address these
issues of stragglers,

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:59.773
we employ a technique called
dynamic work rebalancing.

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:04.050
So how does it work?

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:07.080
So looking again on our
graph with the straggler,

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:14.490
we can see our completion
time of this job or stage

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:16.890
will be once the
red chart finishes,

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:19.740
once the red task finishes.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:20.560
So what can we do?

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:21.720
How can we address this?

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:25.110
So first step in this
is straggler detection.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:26.790
You first have to
identify a problem

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.530
so we can solve it right?

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:33.840
So what Dataflow does, it's
on continually monitors

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:35.300
amount of remaining work.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:38.910
So imagine we're on
this gray line that

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:40.350
shows our current time.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:44.910
And at that time, we check
what's the remaining work.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:50.120
This would be our completion
time if we didn't do anything.

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.300
So we'd run our
algorithm to estimate

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.270
how much work is remaining,
and we come up with this should

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:58.070
be our average completion time.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:00.560
If everything was
perfectly parallelizable

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:03.620
and we were able to
utilize all eight workers,

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:07.090
this is when our
job would finish.

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:10.000
So then, once we have
this information,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.880
a worker can say,
huh, I'm behind.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:15.430
So what I can do is split.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:18.910
I can return fraction of my
work so other can work on it,

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:23.530
and I can finish what
I have in a given time.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:27.380
And now we rebalance the work,
and the situation looks better.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:32.110
It's not perfect, but we did
save some amount of time,

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:34.450
and their new completion
time is there.

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:36.440
Clearly, there is
more work to do.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.950
We should be able to paralyze
this further, so guess what?

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:39.450
We do.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:42.220
So we repeat this
process over and over

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:46.269
during the execution of the
job, and your workers constantly

00:16:46.269 --> 00:16:47.310
get filled with the work.

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:51.932
So what's the impact
on the real job?

00:16:51.932 --> 00:16:53.390
This is again, the
same application

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:56.330
we saw, like the 400 workers
spending a lot of time

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:57.740
waiting on others.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:00.290
And this is how this application
looked like once we enabled

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:02.120
this dynamic work rebalancing.

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:05.300
So we see they're five minutes
out of 20, it's a quarter,

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.940
but this is a five minutes
out of 400 workers,

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.910
like 33 hours of CPU time.

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:12.560
So it's another small saving.

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:20.540
Now we've seen how dynamic work
rebalancing reduces straggler

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:23.240
tail and keeps worker busy.

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:24.829
But it's not the
only thing we do.

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:28.424
Autoscaling allows us
to match the number

00:17:28.424 --> 00:17:30.590
of workers to the throughput
your application needs.

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:35.640
So it scales the number
of workers automatically

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.280
to ensure the pipeline is
keeping up with the demand.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.560
It applies to both
batch and streaming,

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.930
but my example on this
graph is streaming.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.150
So imagine your application
is up and running.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.610
It has some kind of
a QPS, and we run it

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:50.370
with certain number of machines,
in this case having 12,

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:52.860
and things are going fine.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:56.340
As the QPS increases, as
you are getting more hits

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:59.490
on your service, we will
grow the number of workers

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:02.610
to maintain the lag
of processing low.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:06.480
And once the QPS
rate comes down,

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:07.980
we decrease the
number of machines.

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:10.155
Because there is no need
for them to be there,

00:18:10.155 --> 00:18:12.780
you can keep up with the traffic
just with much fewer machines.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:22.710
So how does the autoscaling play
out in a bad job, for example?

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.160
So we start the job, we
do some initial analysis,

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.270
like I said, during
the optimization,

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.540
and we come up with
a, we should run

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:32.670
this with the three workers.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:36.150
And things start running,
and things look OK.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.856
But again, we are continually
estimating when we will finish.

00:18:39.856 --> 00:18:41.730
And at some point, we
have enough information

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:45.390
to conclude that this is
going to take much longer.

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:48.120
We should really be
working with 100 workers.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:50.310
So great, OK, bring
up 100 workers.

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:54.570
The beauty of GCP and
easily scale up, right?

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:56.850
However, the
problem in this case

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:59.910
is we don't have
100 pieces of work.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.620
We initially split
it into like 6 or 9,

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:05.040
whatever, because we thought
we needed three workers.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:08.550
And this is where the dynamic
worker balancing kicks in,

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.430
because now, you
have 100 workers

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:13.560
and you can generate
work for them

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:16.026
by using dynamic
work rebalancing.

00:19:16.026 --> 00:19:17.400
And this is actually
pretty cool.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:20.970
We call it autotuning, like many
people call autotuning things.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:24.460
And you can scale up and
scale down as needed.

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:27.900
This is a job that started
with tens of workers

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:34.450
and then decided that
they needed 1,000.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:37.050
And the blue lines are
showing, essentially,

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.954
when the dynamic work
rebalancing happened.

00:19:39.954 --> 00:19:41.370
Like, these are
the points in time

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.880
when we decided to split the
work and distribute it further.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:48.780
And you can really see
that its going in stages.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:51.390
But the framework is
essentially learning

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:55.710
what your application is doing
in a sense, and adapting to it.

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:05.280
Cool, so why does
this matter to you?

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:09.060
Well, because it helps the
real applications run better,

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.530
I hope.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:14.090
So we will look at a case study.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.920
There is this
application that one

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:20.130
of our sister teams in
Cloud ML cares about a lot,

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.880
so we're monitoring what
that application is doing.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:26.600
And it's a standard ML
preprocessing pipeline.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:30.360
It's applicable to
pretty much any ETL.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.270
You take a data in some
format and you convert it

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:35.910
into format you want.

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:39.210
So this particular
application takes the data

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.970
in tab separated value
format, and converts it

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:45.390
into tensor flow
record format and input

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:47.640
data or click-through
logs from Criteo I think.

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:49.590
It's a Public data set.

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.950
And the pipeline is
reasonably straightforward.

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:56.130
This is our pipeline
graph from Dataflow UI.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:59.100
So we have a stage
that reads from text.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:01.260
Then we decode it.

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:05.520
And then we have this meet stage
called analyze and transform.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:08.340
And then we encode the
output and we write it,

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:10.860
and we make a small side
output with some metadata

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:13.590
there, just store some
information about the run.

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:18.650
This is a
preprocessing step that

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:21.080
can apply to many, many users.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.540
And those users will
differ on input sizes

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:25.330
and the content of the files.

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:28.190
And they will also differ
on analysis parameters

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:29.570
they want to do.

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:31.280
Like, this analyze
and transfer step

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.410
is the meat of transformation.

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:35.180
And it has some
things, like what

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:37.790
is the threshold for
merging the things?

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:40.740
How big of, you know,
output files you want?

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:42.280
Things like that.

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:47.580
And users may want to configure
the application differently.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.130
These are not tuning parameters.

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:53.980
These are application parameters
that affect what the output is.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:56.950
So how would you
tune application

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:59.560
to run the best it can,
given that the users can set

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:02.370
all of these different things?

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:03.290
It's a trick question.

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:04.114
You don't have to.

00:22:04.114 --> 00:22:05.030
You're using Dataflow.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:11.200
OK, so a bit more of context--

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.170
so machine learning
workflows, force parse data,

00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:15.940
typically have three steps.

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:19.390
You preprocess data, you
train, and you serve the model.

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:22.640
And in this case, we are looking
only at the click-through rate

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:23.590
prediction.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:27.970
But if you wanted to do
things like recommendation,

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:29.980
you would have a
different input types

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:33.610
that you would want to add,
things like the inventory,

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.452
or user information.

00:22:35.452 --> 00:22:36.910
And you would have
to perform join,

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:38.460
which would make it
much more complicated

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.876
pipeline than the one that we
are looking in this example.

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:46.510
And for more data
details, you can see

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:49.090
many of the next ML sessions.

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:50.650
And actually, the
code of application

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:53.500
is a PythonSDK, Beam Python.

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:57.370
And it's available in GitHub.

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.530
OK, again, reflection
on a pipeline,

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:04.300
read, decode, analyze and
transform, encode and write.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:08.360
So running this pipeline,
let's look how it looks like.

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.450
So we have a 10 gigabyte input.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.670
It's a fraction of a data set.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:15.550
And this is how
the worker profile

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:16.600
looks for this pipeline.

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:19.630
So on the x-axis, we have time.

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:21.940
This is run from a
week ago or something.

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.850
On the y-axis, we have
number of workers.

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:26.890
And as the pipeline
starts, our initial

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:30.640
estimated that it should have
around 40 workers for reading.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.490
And then the blue
line actually shows

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.270
the workers, actual workers.

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:37.780
The green line is our
estimate and what we want,

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:40.570
what the system is
asking to happen.

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:44.320
So the blue line ramps up to 40.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.180
And then it runs,
runs, runs, and figures

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:49.300
that I can use a little
bit more parallelism here.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:50.950
So it jumps up, and
then once it gets

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:54.490
to a bigger stage, like
analyze transform thing,

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:55.930
it jumps up a lot.

00:23:55.930 --> 00:24:00.010
And it goes from like around
50 workers to 107 or 108.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:02.390
And then it finishes
and it's done.

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:05.630
So this job was around
21 minutes 20 seconds,

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:10.010
and it used 38 CPU hours
and 144 gigabytes of RAM

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:13.760
and five terabytes of PD hours.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:19.340
Now the same input but with a
different threshold parameters

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:20.310
for the pipeline.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:23.780
So this is
application parameter.

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:26.300
Like, I want you to produce
more detailed results.

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:28.570
So it makes it more
expensive to run it,

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:31.620
or more computational expense.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:35.090
So this is how that
one looks like.

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:36.650
It looks similar, right?

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:40.370
But the differences are,
the y-axis now goes to 150

00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:42.440
and above.

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:45.400
And again, we start with a
maybe slightly higher estimate.

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:46.130
We keep going.

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:47.960
There is, again, that bump
when we figure out there

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:49.460
might be some more
parallelism here.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:50.750
Let me utilize it.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:53.150
And then once we get
to the expensive stage,

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:56.570
we just ramp up high
and then add some more.

00:24:56.570 --> 00:25:00.070
And we run it like 173 workers.

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:04.270
And the runtime is almost the
same, 22 minutes 17 seconds.

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:06.430
But you end up using
way more resources,

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:12.100
61 CPU hours, 230 gigabytes
of RAM, and 8 terabytes of PD.

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:14.680
My point here is not
that this was more

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:16.210
computationally intensive.

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:19.270
My point is that it was the same
common line as far as Dataflow

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:20.740
is concerned.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:23.350
The only change
was in parameters.

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:27.040
And we were able to
keep all these workers

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.705
busy for the duration
of the pipeline.

00:25:30.705 --> 00:25:33.730
They were utilized at 100% in
terms of what they are doing.

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:39.270
So now we want to look
at the larger scale.

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.340
The full input size
is one terabyte.

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:43.200
What does that do to the graph?

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.500
There are a few changes.

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:49.550
First, our y-axis goes to 1,000.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.020
This is a system limit--

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:52.820
and I'm going to show it later--

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:56.980
and when we initially started,
we figured out between 500,

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:58.520
600 workers would be good.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.740
So we again start
running the first part.

00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:04.040
We had the bump, like we think
there is more parallelism here,

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:05.870
but then we were like,
no, we were good.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:07.460
So we came back down.

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:11.480
And then we have this part,
which actually corresponds

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:14.180
to a group by key step.

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:15.790
What happens in a
group by key step

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:21.310
is you collect all the
data from incoming charts,

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:24.670
and then you have to
group them by key.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:27.190
And this operation is
sort of two-step one.

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:30.970
And you cannot proceed the next
stage until you are fully done.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:33.550
Because you need to be sure
that you saw all the keys.

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:35.590
So what happened here
is that the group by key

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:38.740
couldn't really fully keep
up with what was coming in,

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:40.210
so it had to finish.

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:42.490
Now, they do use dynamic
work rebalancing,

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:45.650
but there are always more
opportunities for improving,

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:47.360
and this is one of them.

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:50.260
So what happened here is,
while we were finishing

00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:56.450
the tasks that
weren't paral further,

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:57.970
we shut down the other ones.

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:00.880
There was no reason for these
machines to sit there idle,

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:04.430
because they
couldn't do anything.

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:07.340
And then once that
step was done,

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:09.590
we shoot up in parallelism
and we were using

00:27:09.590 --> 00:27:11.690
the system imposed maximum.

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:16.220
We do not go above 1,000
workers unless you tell us.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:21.290
We think it's a reasonable
maximum to have by default.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:25.880
OK, so this process, this
job, took one hour 30 minutes

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:28.610
and used quite a
bit of resources.

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:33.050
And in terms of scalability, the
input ratio size was 100 times,

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:36.230
the CPU ratio size
was 104 times.

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:40.760
In terms of gigabyte RAMs
used, it was 104 times as well.

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:44.320
And the PD usage was 52 times x.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:47.170
And the wall time ratio was 4.2.

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:50.260
The ideal scalability in this
case would be one, right?

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:53.387
But Amdahl's law
is pretty strong.

00:27:53.387 --> 00:27:54.970
So this part where
we had to shut down

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:58.490
and slowdown really does
affect the performance.

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:00.354
But we don't waste resources.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:06.840
Now, let's look at the
performance of this application

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:07.920
over the last six months.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:11.310
Again, it's important
application to the sister team.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:14.740
And we are with them
monitoring what's happening.

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:18.390
So the red line shows the
runtime over a six months

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:21.210
period.

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:25.680
The initial drop there was
a bunch of data flow level

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.970
improvements that we did on the
Python SDK, or Beam Python SDK,

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:31.600
after [? GL ?] launch.

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.860
So we spent time improving the
connectors and these things,

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:38.160
and that had a very visible
impact on the runtime

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:39.210
of the performance.

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:44.560
Note that the application did
not change in this time period.

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:45.990
So these are all
the improvements

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:48.070
that are happening,
or all the changes

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:50.820
were happening from
system perspective,

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:52.520
from tool perspective.

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:58.110
And this here, we
enabled the autoscaling.

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:03.570
And that was another
drop in a duration time.

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:06.120
And then at this time someone
had the brilliant idea

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:09.270
that, hey, we are looking at
the resource usage and this.

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:12.150
What does it mean for the cost?

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:14.510
So we start tracking how
much the application costs

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:15.750
at that point.

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:20.100
And I think in that moment, we
were running at 62 minutes wall

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:24.800
time, and it cost around $225.

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:27.590
And end of January,
we were at 57 minutes,

00:29:27.590 --> 00:29:31.100
so there were no major
reduction in the vault time,

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:33.461
but our price is
significantly lower.

00:29:33.461 --> 00:29:35.210
And what this tells
you is that the system

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:37.420
became more efficient.

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:39.590
Maybe your vault time
that much, but you

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:41.251
are using less resources.

00:29:41.251 --> 00:29:42.500
And it's the same application.

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:46.710
They're running it
continually, essentially.

00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:49.080
And I think this is like
one of the core things

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:51.300
that I would like you
to come out with this,

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:54.240
is we really care about the
cost effective performance.

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.760
And we treat it as a first
class feature of the system.

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:04.630
And with that, we'll
switch the gears

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:07.120
and look at our Cloud
Dataproc product.

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.021
We will look at how
the system level

00:30:14.021 --> 00:30:16.270
improvements affect the
performance in Cloud Dataproc.

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:22.000
So what is Cloud Dataproc?

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:26.340
Dataproc brings the widely used
Apache Hadoop and Apache Spark

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:27.880
ecosystems to GCP.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:31.120
It is fully managed, it's
easy to use, it's fast,

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:32.247
and it's cost effective.

00:30:32.247 --> 00:30:33.580
And I'll talk about all of them.

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:42.540
So how does the Cloud Dataproc
cluster lifecycle looks like?

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:46.900
Well, when you need to
you start one cluster.

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:49.450
You then use it.

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:53.680
And you'd run
Spark, Hadoop, Hive,

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:59.200
any of these Apache open
source ecosystem tools,

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:01.390
and then when you're
done you shut it down.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:03.020
You delete the cluster.

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:05.830
So it doesn't use your time.

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:08.909
While you're using it, you
can still modify the cluster.

00:31:08.909 --> 00:31:11.200
So you know, you start to
with a big cluster with like,

00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:13.570
20 machines, whatever,
now you need 50.

00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:15.940
And you can pretty
simply change that

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:20.630
to a bigger size or smaller
size, depending what you need.

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:22.612
So this is exciting.

00:31:22.612 --> 00:31:25.070
I mean, if you think you bring
up your cluster, you use it,

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:28.850
you shut it down, there
is some like power to it

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:30.410
when you do it.

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:34.670
But like, often Spark and Hadoop
systems are slow to deploy

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:36.410
and they're costly and complex.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:41.052
So start, use, and
delete sounds, you know,

00:31:41.052 --> 00:31:43.010
wasteful if you're going
to spend a lot of time

00:31:43.010 --> 00:31:44.540
starting the things.

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:48.740
But that's not the case
with the Dataproc, right?

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.310
Oh yeah, and we do
one-minute billing.

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:55.730
So starting a Cloud Dataproc
is as simple, literally,

00:31:55.730 --> 00:32:00.130
as going to have a page,
clicking Create cluster,

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:04.630
optionally configuring
it, and doing start.

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:07.040
And within a 90 seconds
from the beginning,

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:10.822
you can have a machine
sped up, fully configured.

00:32:10.822 --> 00:32:12.280
Whenever you need
to change it, you

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:15.987
go back to configuration page
and you change the parameters

00:32:15.987 --> 00:32:16.570
that you want.

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:21.550
So the major goal of
Dataproc is to bring

00:32:21.550 --> 00:32:23.630
the power of Google
Cloud Platform

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:27.040
to Spark and Hadoop users.

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:29.215
The team builds connectors
and integrations

00:32:29.215 --> 00:32:32.020
with other Cloud products,
like BigQuery, Cloud Storage,

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:34.960
Cloud Bigtable, Cloud
Spanner, Compute Engine,

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:40.810
and allows you to read and
write from these seemingly.

00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:43.090
One of the example
jobs that they have

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:46.660
is like PySpark job that
takes the data from GCS,

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:49.850
uses a PySpark, and then produce
the output in a BigQuery.

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:55.690
So how does it look
like under the hood?

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:58.148
So we have our clients on the
left hand side and the Google

00:32:58.148 --> 00:32:59.430
Cloud Platform on the right.

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:04.920
And Dataproc exposes
the rest APIs

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:07.740
for managing the cluster,
for managing the jobs,

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:11.060
and other operations
that you may want to do.

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:14.090
So you can access
these APIs through UI,

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:16.310
or you can script
against them, like, you

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:19.400
can program against this API.

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:22.310
Next, Cloud Dataproc
has the clusters

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:25.370
that will build on top
of compute engines.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:29.280
And there is this Dataproc
agent that manages--

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:32.190
it's sort of control plane--
and it manages your cluster,

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:35.570
makes sure that VMs are
up and things like that.

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:40.820
And this also, the
image that we have,

00:33:40.820 --> 00:33:42.920
is built on top of Big Top.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:46.880
So it comes with Spark, Hadoop,
Hive, and many other things

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:48.080
that are there.

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:51.020
When the cluster is
brought up, we also

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:54.200
bring up some persistent
disks and we install the HDFS

00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:59.390
on them, or we make them
available through HDFS.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:03.260
However, if you want to use
the start, use, stop cycle,

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:06.230
you cannot really keep the
input and outputs on the HDFS,

00:34:06.230 --> 00:34:08.330
because it goes away
when you're done.

00:34:08.330 --> 00:34:12.260
So what you do instead is you
put your inputs and outputs

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:15.350
on Cloud Storage, in BigQuery,
on one of these services that

00:34:15.350 --> 00:34:21.310
are always up but do not come
down and up with your cluster.

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:30.010
So at some point on a
bright day last year,

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:32.770
in our regular performance
analysis things,

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:35.110
we noticed a problem.

00:34:35.110 --> 00:34:40.340
And the problem was
that for big jobs,

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:43.429
accessing a big amount
of data on GCS, the read

00:34:43.429 --> 00:34:48.380
and write were spending a lot
of time in SSL related code,

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:51.230
so much more that it wasn't
a bottlenecked on network,

00:34:51.230 --> 00:34:53.949
but it was bottlenecked
on CPU operations.

00:34:53.949 --> 00:34:56.780
Like, we're bottlenecked on CPU.

00:34:56.780 --> 00:35:00.220
Remember I said that we
recommend GCS store users,

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:03.390
so this was surprising.

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:05.870
So we analyzed it and
determined the Java

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:10.340
based SSL implementation
is very CPU bound.

00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:14.160
And once we identified
the problem,

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:15.830
we could come up
with a [? fifty ?]

00:35:15.830 --> 00:35:18.950
solution momentarily that
would help the people who

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:21.500
are affected make it run
better, but we really

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:23.660
wanted to do it in
a systematic way

00:35:23.660 --> 00:35:26.600
to make it available
to all of our users.

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:31.790
And that meant that we needed
a system level solution that

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:34.100
will be deployed to everyone
and will make everyone's

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:37.430
life better, not just the
few that were complaining

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.362
that things are slow.

00:35:40.362 --> 00:35:41.320
So what's the solution?

00:35:43.830 --> 00:35:47.450
The solution was to switch
implementation to BoringSSL.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:49.680
And because it's BoringSSL,
it's boring name,

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:51.705
I had to have a
unicorn that sparkle.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:36:00.280
So what it involved, well,
quite a bit of things.

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:03.660
But essentially, over
the next few weeks,

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:06.990
the old Dataproc customers
will have significantly

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:12.240
improved read and write
performance to GCS.

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:14.490
And essentially,
these are the results.

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:17.790
So we're looking at the
TestDFSIO benchmark.

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:20.280
x-axis is throughput.

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:23.510
The more the merrier, so you
want a higher throughput.

00:36:23.510 --> 00:36:26.700
And light blue is
the old method,

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:30.000
and dark blue is the
BoringSSL method.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.960
And essentially, we are
seeing 2 and 1/2 times faster

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:37.560
reads, and around
20% faster writes,

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:39.030
and there are no user changes.

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:42.540
It's just like the new system
configuration that runs.

00:36:42.540 --> 00:36:45.370
And the different jobs will
see different benefits,

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:47.920
because if your job wasn't
bottlenecked on this,

00:36:47.920 --> 00:36:50.070
this is not going to
significantly improve it,

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:53.010
because your end time depends
on more than just this.

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:56.940
But this is another example of
like, systematic improvement

00:36:56.940 --> 00:36:59.010
that goes in a system,
and if you were watching

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:02.877
your application over
time, will make it cheaper

00:37:02.877 --> 00:37:03.960
and more efficient to run.

00:37:09.490 --> 00:37:12.550
So we'll have some
takeaways now.

00:37:16.790 --> 00:37:18.860
So when trying to achieve
the performance and you

00:37:18.860 --> 00:37:21.050
want to do that automatically
for data processing

00:37:21.050 --> 00:37:25.220
applications, the tools have
to look at the different types

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:26.420
of factors.

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:29.360
We had application level
factors, environmental factors,

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:31.010
and system factors.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:33.950
We try to systematically
address all three of them

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:37.490
across both of these products
and across those GCP.

00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:39.080
Performance is
something we care about

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:40.850
and we look at all the time.

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:44.300
We have dedicated
people for that.

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:47.360
In our talk, we covered
the application aspects

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:49.160
and the environment
aspects and how

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:50.930
we deal with them
through Dataflow,

00:37:50.930 --> 00:37:52.730
looking at the dynamic
work rebalancing,

00:37:52.730 --> 00:37:55.580
autoscaling the graph
optimizations we do.

00:37:55.580 --> 00:38:00.230
And on the system side, we'll
look at Dataproc and how

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:02.900
they improved one of the
core system libraries

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:07.214
by replacing it,
essentially, to increase

00:38:07.214 --> 00:38:08.630
the performance
of read and writes

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:11.750
to GCS, which is a very common
pattern that our users use.

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:20.550
So data processing tools
help your application achieve

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:22.950
performance automatically.

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:25.110
We systematically
tackle different factors

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:27.090
that affect the performance.

00:38:27.090 --> 00:38:29.130
And we provide real
time autotuning

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:31.500
in Cloud Dataflow and
system level improvements

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:32.250
in Cloud Dataproc.

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:40.870
And we make improvements to the
system and tools continually.

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:43.135
So we take the performance
available to you

00:38:43.135 --> 00:38:46.030
out of the box, and then
no knobs are needed.

00:38:46.030 --> 00:38:50.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

